  "art1_title": "Healthy Eating in the Second Trimester",
  "@art1_title": {
    "description": "Article 1 title – healthy eating in trimester 2"
  },
  "art1_tagNutrisi": "Nutrition",
  "@art1_tagNutrisi": {
    "description": "Article 1 tag"
  },
  "art1_tagTrimester2": "Trimester 2",
  "@art1_tagTrimester2": {
    "description": "Article 1 tag"
  },
  "art1_intro": "The second trimester is often called the 'golden period' of pregnancy — morning sickness usually fades, energy starts to return, and your bump begins to show. It's also the perfect time to pay close attention to what's on your plate, as your baby is growing rapidly and needs more nutrients than ever before.",
  "@art1_intro": {
    "description": "Opening paragraph of article 1"
  },
  "art1_callout": "A pregnant woman's calorie needs increase by around ==340 kcal per day== in the second trimester compared to before pregnancy. But the quality of food matters far more than the quantity.",
  "@art1_callout": {
    "description": "Callout for article 1"
  },
  "art1_sectionNutrisi": "Key Nutrients in Trimester 2",
  "@art1_sectionNutrisi": {
    "description": "Section heading for article 1"
  },
  "art1_item1_title": "Protein for your baby's growth",
  "@art1_item1_title": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 1 title"
  },
  "art1_item1_content": "Protein is the main building block for your baby's cells and tissues. During this trimester, your protein needs increase by around 25 grams per day. Best sources: eggs, fish, chicken, tofu, tempeh, and legumes.",
  "@art1_item1_content": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 1 content"
  },
  "art1_item2_title": "Calcium and vitamin D for strong bones",
  "@art1_item2_title": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 2 title"
  },
  "art1_item2_content": "Your baby's bones begin to harden significantly in the second trimester. Calcium from milk, yogurt, broccoli, and dried anchovies, combined with vitamin D from morning sunlight, supports optimal bone development.",
  "@art1_item2_content": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 2 content"
  },
  "art1_item3_title": "Omega-3 for brain development",
  "@art1_item3_title": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 3 title"
  },
  "art1_item3_content": "Your baby's brain is developing very actively this trimester. ==DHA and EPA== from salmon, anchovies, or fish oil supplements help support your baby's nervous system and vision development.",
  "@art1_item3_content": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 3 content"
  },
  "art1_item4_title": "Iron to prevent anaemia",
  "@art1_item4_title": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 4 title"
  },
  "art1_item4_content": "Your blood volume increases by up to ==50%== during pregnancy, so your iron needs rise as well. Eat lean red meat, spinach, and kidney beans. Avoid drinking tea or coffee alongside iron-rich foods as they can inhibit absorption.",
  "@art1_item4_content": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 4 content"
  },
  "art1_item5_title": "Fibre to keep digestion smooth",
  "@art1_item5_title": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 5 title"
  },
  "art1_item5_content": "Constipation is a common complaint in the second trimester due to hormonal changes and the expanding uterus. Green vegetables, fruits, and whole grains help keep your digestive system moving. Don't forget to drink enough water.",
  "@art1_item5_content": {
    "description": "Article 1 item 5 content"
  },
  "art1_sectionTips": "Everyday Healthy Eating Tips",
  "@art1_sectionTips": {
    "description": "Section heading tips for article 1"
  },
  "art1_tips_intro": "Eating well doesn't have to be complicated. A few small habits can make a big difference:",
  "@art1_tips_intro": {
    "description": "Tips intro for article 1"
  },
  "art1_tips_item1_title": "Split meals into 5–6 smaller portions",
  "@art1_tips_item1_title": {
    "description": "Article 1 tips item 1 title"
  },
  "art1_tips_item1_a": "Eating more frequently in smaller portions helps keep blood sugar levels stable",
  "@art1_tips_item1_a": {
    "description": "Article 1 tips 1a"
  },
  "art1_tips_item1_b": "Avoid going too long on an empty stomach to prevent dizziness or fatigue",
  "@art1_tips_item1_b": {
    "description": "Article 1 tips 1b"
  },
  "art1_tips_item1_c": "Sneak in healthy snacks like fruit, nuts, or yogurt between meals",
  "@art1_tips_item1_c": {
    "description": "Article 1 tips 1c"
  },
  "art1_tips_item2_title": "Stay well hydrated",
  "@art1_tips_item2_title": {
    "description": "Article 1 tips item 2 title"
  },
  "art1_tips_item2_a": "Aim for 8–10 glasses of water per day to support blood volume and amniotic fluid",
  "@art1_tips_item2_a": {
    "description": "Article 1 tips 2a"
  },
  "art1_tips_item2_b": "Plain water is the best choice; limit sugary and caffeinated drinks",
  "@art1_tips_item2_b": {
    "description": "Article 1 tips 2b"
  },
  "art1_sectionRef": "References",
  "@art1_sectionRef": {
    "description": "Section heading references for article 1"
  },
  "art1_ref1": "World Health Organization (WHO). Nutrition during pregnancy. who.int",
  "@art1_ref1": {
    "description": "Article 1 reference 1"
  },
  "art1_ref2": "American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG). Nutrition during pregnancy. acog.org",
  "@art1_ref2": {
    "description": "Article 1 reference 2"
  },
  "art1_ref3": "Mayo Clinic. Pregnancy diet: Focus on these essential nutrients. mayoclinic.org",
  "@art1_ref3": {
    "description": "Article 1 reference 3"
  },
  "art2_title": "Safe Exercise During Pregnancy",
  "@art2_title": {
    "description": "Article 2 title – safe exercise during pregnancy"
  },
  "art2_tagAktivitas": "Activity",
  "@art2_tagAktivitas": {
    "description": "Article 2 tag"
  },
  "art2_tagKesehatan": "Health",
  "@art2_tagKesehatan": {
    "description": "Article 2 tag"
  },
  "art2_intro": "There used to be a belief that pregnant women should rest as much as possible and avoid physical activity. Research now proves otherwise — regular exercise done correctly is actually very beneficial for both mother and baby, from maintaining weight to preparing the body for labour.",
  "@art2_intro": {
    "description": "Opening paragraph of article 2"
  },
  "art2_callout": "General guideline: 150 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity per week is enough for healthy pregnant women — equivalent to 30 minutes of brisk walking 5 days a week.",
  "@art2_callout": {
    "description": "Callout for article 2"
  },
  "art2_sectionJenis": "Recommended Types of Exercise",
  "@art2_sectionJenis": {
    "description": "Section heading for article 2"
  },
  "art2_item1_title": "Walking",
  "@art2_item1_title": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 1 title"
  },
  "art2_item1_a": "The easiest and safest activity at any trimester",
  "@art2_item1_a": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 1a"
  },
  "art2_item1_b": "Start with 15–20 minutes and gradually build up to 30 minutes",
  "@art2_item1_b": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 1b"
  },
  "art2_item1_c": "Wear comfortable shoes and avoid uneven surfaces",
  "@art2_item1_c": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 1c"
  },
  "art2_item2_title": "Prenatal exercise classes",
  "@art2_item2_title": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 2 title"
  },
  "art2_item2_a": "Designed specifically for pregnant women and safe at any stage of pregnancy",
  "@art2_item2_a": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 2a"
  },
  "art2_item2_b": "Helps train pelvic muscles and breathing in preparation for labour",
  "@art2_item2_b": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 2b"
  },
  "art2_item2_c": "Available in in-person classes or online video guides",
  "@art2_item2_c": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 2c"
  },
  "art2_item3_title": "Prenatal yoga",
  "@art2_item3_title": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 3 title"
  },
  "art2_item3_a": "Trains flexibility, balance, and breathing all at once",
  "@art2_item3_a": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 3a"
  },
  "art2_item3_b": "Meditation movements help reduce anxiety and stress",
  "@art2_item3_b": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 3b"
  },
  "art2_item3_c": "Choose a class led by a certified prenatal instructor",
  "@art2_item3_c": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 3c"
  },
  "art2_item4_title": "Swimming",
  "@art2_item4_title": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 4 title"
  },
  "art2_item4_a": "Water supports your body weight, making it very comfortable — especially in the third trimester",
  "@art2_item4_a": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 4a"
  },
  "art2_item4_b": "Works the whole body without stressing the joints",
  "@art2_item4_b": {
    "description": "Article 2 item 4b"
  },
  "art2_sectionPantangan": "What to Avoid",
  "@art2_sectionPantangan": {
    "description": "Section heading avoid for article 2"
  },
  "art2_pantangan_callout": "Stop exercising immediately and contact your doctor if you experience chest pain, excessive breathlessness, dizziness, contractions, or fluid leaking from the vagina.",
  "@art2_pantangan_callout": {
    "description": "Warning callout for article 2"
  },
  "art2_pantangan1_title": "Sports with a risk of falling or impact",
  "@art2_pantangan1_title": {
    "description": "Article 2 avoid item 1"
  },
  "art2_pantangan1_content": "Avoid sports such as road cycling, horse riding, skiing, or contact sports. The risk of falling or receiving a blow to the abdomen is very dangerous for the baby.",
  "@art2_pantangan1_content": {
    "description": "Article 2 avoid item 1 content"
  },
  "art2_pantangan2_title": "Lying flat on your back for too long",
  "@art2_pantangan2_title": {
    "description": "Article 2 avoid item 2"
  },
  "art2_pantangan2_content": "After the first trimester, avoid exercises that require lying flat on your back for more than a few minutes. This position can compress the major blood vessel (==inferior vena cava==) and reduce blood flow to your baby.",
  "@art2_pantangan2_content": {
    "description": "Article 2 avoid item 2 content"
  },
  "art2_pantangan3_title": "Exercising in hot or humid weather",
  "@art2_pantangan3_title": {
    "description": "Article 2 avoid item 3"
  },
  "art2_pantangan3_content": "A pregnant woman's body temperature rises more easily. Avoid exercising outdoors in hot weather, and make sure there is good ventilation when exercising indoors.",
  "@art2_pantangan3_content": {
    "description": "Article 2 avoid item 3 content"
  },
  "art2_sectionRef": "References",
  "@art2_sectionRef": {
    "description": "Section heading references for article 2"
  },
  "art2_ref1": "American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG). Exercise during pregnancy. acog.org",
  "@art2_ref1": {
    "description": "Article 2 reference 1"
  },
  "art2_ref2": "Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG). Exercise in pregnancy. rcog.org.uk",
  "@art2_ref2": {
    "description": "Article 2 reference 2"
  },
  "art2_ref3": "Mayo Clinic. Pregnancy and exercise: Baby, let's move! mayoclinic.org",
  "@art2_ref3": {
    "description": "Article 2 reference 3"
  },
  "art3_title": "Tips for Sleeping Comfortably in the Third Trimester",
  "@art3_title": {
    "description": "Article 3 title – comfortable sleep in trimester 3"
  },
  "art3_tagPerawatan": "Self-care",
  "@art3_tagPerawatan": {
    "description": "Article 3 tag"
  },
  "art3_tagTrimester3": "Trimester 3",
  "@art3_tagTrimester3": {
    "description": "Article 3 tag"
  },
  "art3_intro": "The third trimester brings many body changes — your bump grows larger, your back starts to ache, and finding a comfortable sleeping position can feel like a challenge in itself. Sleep disturbances at this stage are very common, but that doesn't mean nothing can be done about them.",
  "@art3_intro": {
    "description": "Opening paragraph of article 3"
  },
  "art3_callout": "Good, restful sleep is not a luxury — it's a basic need for pregnant women. Prolonged sleep deprivation can increase the risk of pregnancy complications and slow down recovery after birth.",
  "@art3_callout": {
    "description": "Callout for article 3"
  },
  "art3_sectionPosisi": "The Best Sleeping Positions",
  "@art3_sectionPosisi": {
    "description": "Section heading for article 3"
  },
  "art3_item1_title": "Sleep on your left side (SOS — Sleep On Side)",
  "@art3_item1_title": {
    "description": "Article 3 item 1 title"
  },
  "art3_item1_content": "Sleeping on your left side is the most recommended position in the third trimester. It improves blood flow to the placenta and kidneys, and reduces pressure on your liver. If you wake up in a different position, just roll back to the left — don't worry too much if your position shifts during sleep.",
  "@art3_item1_content": {
    "description": "Article 3 item 1 content"
  },
  "art3_item2_title": "Use extra pillows",
  "@art3_item2_title": {
    "description": "Article 3 item 2 title"
  },
  "art3_item2_content": "Place a pillow between your knees to keep your hips aligned and reduce back pain. A long body pillow or bolster can be a great sleeping companion. A thin pillow under your bump also helps support the weight of the baby.",
  "@art3_item2_content": {
    "description": "Article 3 item 2 content"
  },
  "art3_item3_title": "Avoid lying flat on your back",
  "@art3_item3_title": {
    "description": "Article 3 item 3 title"
  },
  "art3_item3_content": "Lying flat on your back for extended periods in the third trimester can compress the ==inferior vena cava==, which may cause dizziness, nausea, or reduced blood flow to your baby. If you occasionally wake up on your back, don't panic — just gently roll back onto your side.",
  "@art3_item3_content": {
    "description": "Article 3 item 3 content"
  },
  "art3_sectionTips": "Tips for Better Sleep",
  "@art3_sectionTips": {
    "description": "Section heading tips for article 3"
  },
  "art3_tips1_title": "Create a consistent sleep routine",
  "@art3_tips1_title": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 1 title"
  },
  "art3_tips1_a": "Go to bed and wake up at the same time every day, including weekends",
  "@art3_tips1_a": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 1a"
  },
  "art3_tips1_b": "Dim the lights 30–60 minutes before bed to signal to your body that it's time to rest",
  "@art3_tips1_b": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 1b"
  },
  "art3_tips1_c": "Avoid phone or tablet screens for at least 30 minutes before sleeping",
  "@art3_tips1_c": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 1c"
  },
  "art3_tips2_title": "Manage complaints that disrupt sleep",
  "@art3_tips2_title": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 2 title"
  },
  "art3_tips2_a": "For heartburn: eat dinner earlier, in smaller portions, and slightly elevate your head while sleeping",
  "@art3_tips2_a": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 2a"
  },
  "art3_tips2_b": "For leg cramps: do light calf stretches before bed and ensure you're getting enough calcium and magnesium",
  "@art3_tips2_b": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 2b"
  },
  "art3_tips2_c": "For frequent urination: reduce fluid intake 2 hours before bed, but make sure your total daily intake remains sufficient",
  "@art3_tips2_c": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 2c"
  },
  "art3_tips3_title": "Keep your sleep environment comfortable",
  "@art3_tips3_title": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 3 title"
  },
  "art3_tips3_a": "A cool room temperature (around 18–22°C) helps your body fall asleep more easily",
  "@art3_tips3_a": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 3a"
  },
  "art3_tips3_b": "Complete darkness or blackout curtains help stimulate melatonin production, your body's natural sleep hormone",
  "@art3_tips3_b": {
    "description": "Article 3 tips 3b"
  },
  "art3_sectionRef": "References",
  "@art3_sectionRef": {
    "description": "Section heading references for article 3"
  },
  "art3_ref1": "National Sleep Foundation. Sleeping positions during pregnancy. sleepfoundation.org",
  "@art3_ref1": {
    "description": "Article 3 reference 1"
  },
  "art3_ref2": "American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG). How to sleep comfortably during pregnancy. acog.org",
  "@art3_ref2": {
    "description": "Article 3 reference 2"
  },
  "art3_ref3": "Mayo Clinic. Sleeping positions during pregnancy. mayoclinic.org",
  "@art3_ref3": {
    "description": "Article 3 reference 3"
  },
  "art4_title": "Managing Stress During Pregnancy",
  "@art4_title": {
    "description": "Article 4 title – managing stress during pregnancy"
  },
  "art4_tagMentalHealth": "Mental Health",
  "@art4_tagMentalHealth": {
    "description": "Article 4 tag"
  },
  "art4_tagPerawatan": "Self-care",
  "@art4_tagPerawatan": {
    "description": "Article 4 tag"
  },
  "art4_intro": "Feeling anxious, worried, or overwhelmed during pregnancy is completely human. Your body and emotions are going through enormous changes all at once, and it's natural to feel like it's a lot to handle. The goal isn't to eliminate stress entirely — it's to learn how to manage it in a healthy way.",
  "@art4_intro": {
    "description": "Opening paragraph of article 4"
  },
  "art4_callout": "Mild to moderate stress is generally not harmful to your baby. However, severe, prolonged stress that goes unaddressed can increase the risk of preterm birth and low birth weight. Don't hesitate to ask for help.",
  "@art4_callout": {
    "description": "Callout for article 4"
  },
  "art4_sectionTanda": "Recognising Signs of Excessive Stress",
  "@art4_sectionTanda": {
    "description": "Section heading for article 4"
  },
  "art4_tanda_intro": "Stress in pregnant women can show up in many forms. Recognising it early is an important first step:",
  "@art4_tanda_intro": {
    "description": "Intro for stress signs in article 4"
  },
  "art4_tanda1_title": "Physical signs",
  "@art4_tanda1_title": {
    "description": "Article 4 sign 1 title"
  },
  "art4_tanda1_content": "Frequent headaches, tension in the shoulders and neck, sleep disturbances, dramatic changes in appetite, or heart palpitations without an obvious cause.",
  "@art4_tanda1_content": {
    "description": "Article 4 sign 1 content"
  },
  "art4_tanda2_title": "Emotional signs",
  "@art4_tanda2_title": {
    "description": "Article 4 sign 2 title"
  },
  "art4_tanda2_content": "Crying easily, feeling helpless, frequent unexplained irritability, difficulty concentrating, or losing interest in things that used to bring you joy.",
  "@art4_tanda2_content": {
    "description": "Article 4 sign 2 content"
  },
  "art4_tanda3_title": "Behavioural signs",
  "@art4_tanda3_title": {
    "description": "Article 4 sign 3 title"
  },
  "art4_tanda3_content": "Withdrawing from people close to you, avoiding social activities, or on the other hand, staying overly busy to distract yourself — both can be signs that stress needs attention.",
  "@art4_tanda3_content": {
    "description": "Article 4 sign 3 content"
  },
  "art4_sectionCara": "Healthy Ways to Manage Stress",
  "@art4_sectionCara": {
    "description": "Section heading for article 4"
  },
  "art4_cara1_title": "Relaxation techniques",
  "@art4_cara1_title": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 1 title"
  },
  "art4_cara1_a": "Deep breathing: inhale for 4 counts, hold for 4, exhale for 6 — repeat 5–10 times",
  "@art4_cara1_a": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 1a"
  },
  "art4_cara1_b": "10 minutes of meditation or mindfulness each morning helps calm your mind before the day begins",
  "@art4_cara1_b": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 1b"
  },
  "art4_cara1_c": "Prenatal yoga combines gentle movement with breathing techniques that are highly effective for easing anxiety",
  "@art4_cara1_c": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 1c"
  },
  "art4_cara2_title": "Stay physically active",
  "@art4_cara2_title": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 2 title"
  },
  "art4_cara2_a": "A 20–30 minute daily walk has been shown to boost endorphin levels and improve mood",
  "@art4_cara2_a": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 2a"
  },
  "art4_cara2_b": "Choose activities you genuinely enjoy — not ones that feel like a chore",
  "@art4_cara2_b": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 2b"
  },
  "art4_cara3_title": "Build a support system",
  "@art4_cara3_title": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 3 title"
  },
  "art4_cara3_a": "Share your feelings with your partner, parents, or a trusted friend — sharing the load makes everything feel lighter",
  "@art4_cara3_a": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 3a"
  },
  "art4_cara3_b": "Join a pregnancy community or mothers' group to share experiences and support one another",
  "@art4_cara3_b": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 3b"
  },
  "art4_cara3_c": "Don't hesitate to ask for practical help — cooking, shopping, or housework — that's not a sign of weakness",
  "@art4_cara3_c": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 3c"
  },
  "art4_cara4_title": "Look after the basics",
  "@art4_cara4_title": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 4 title"
  },
  "art4_cara4_a": "Enough sleep, regular meals, and adequate hydration are the foundations of mental wellbeing that are often underestimated",
  "@art4_cara4_a": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 4a"
  },
  "art4_cara4_b": "If stress feels very heavy or has lasted ==more than 2 weeks==, talk to your midwife or doctor — seeking professional help is a wise choice",
  "@art4_cara4_b": {
    "description": "Article 4 method 4b"
  },
  "art4_sectionRef": "References",
  "@art4_sectionRef": {
    "description": "Section heading references for article 4"
  },
  "art4_ref1": "American Psychological Association (APA). Stress and pregnancy. apa.org",
  "@art4_ref1": {
    "description": "Article 4 reference 1"
  },
  "art4_ref2": "March of Dimes. Stress and pregnancy. marchofdimes.org",
  "@art4_ref2": {
    "description": "Article 4 reference 2"
  },
  "art4_ref3": "NHS. Mental health in pregnancy. nhs.uk",
  "@art4_ref3": {
    "description": "Article 4 reference 3"
  },
